WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.038
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Privileged Access.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:09.888
This is Chapter 8: evidence
and drift for the ISSO.

4
00:00:09.888 --> 00:00:13.637
An inventory taken once only
proves the day it was taken.

5
00:00:13.637 --> 00:00:18.026
The ISSO needs every day of the
period, every elevation in it,

6
00:00:18.026 --> 00:00:21.699
and a record nobody could
have edited afterwards.

7
00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:26.581
We'll start from Learn's audit queries
for PIM, see what they leave out, then

8
00:00:26.581 --> 00:00:31.625
walk the run, the comparison, the package,
the period, two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:31.625 --> 00:00:37.140
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.625
Why does this chapter exist?

11
00:00:43.625 --> 00:00:48.549
Because the program's last gates ask for
things a single snapshot can't give: a

12
00:00:48.549 --> 00:00:53.863
Tier 0 drift handled as an incident, and
evidence for every elevation in the period.

13
00:00:53.863 --> 00:00:58.537
The workbook's privileged-access design
already says what goes in the package.

14
00:00:58.537 --> 00:01:01.576
Learn supplies the two
facts the design turns on.

15
00:01:01.576 --> 00:01:04.482
First, the audit log
only keeps thirty days,

16
00:01:04.482 --> 00:01:07.887
so evidence has to be
collected before it ages out.

17
00:01:07.887 --> 00:01:12.563
Second, one activation can show
up under several correlation IDs,

18
00:01:12.563 --> 00:01:14.901
but its request ID never changes.

19
00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:17.550
That request ID becomes the key.

20
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.663
Next, which cloud.

21
00:01:22.663 --> 00:01:27.263
The new read in this chapter is the
directory audit log in Microsoft Graph.

22
00:01:27.263 --> 00:01:31.241
This estate's GCC tenant uses
the worldwide Graph endpoint,

23
00:01:31.241 --> 00:01:35.463
and its Azure reads go to the
commercial Resource Manager host.

24
00:01:35.463 --> 00:01:40.158
GCC High and the Department of Defense
clouds have their own Graph hosts,

25
00:01:40.158 --> 00:01:44.619
and Learn lists the audit API as
available in both government levels,

26
00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:46.888
with PIM available everywhere too.

27
00:01:46.888 --> 00:01:51.160
As in every chapter, the cloud is
a required choice with no default,

28
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.930
and the reader refuses to
send a token anywhere else.

29
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.392
Here's what Microsoft gives you.

30
00:02:00.392 --> 00:02:05.404
Learn's audit history page shows PIM's own
audit view, says it covers thirty days,

31
00:02:05.404 --> 00:02:10.185
and gives three sample queries: one for
role activations, one that follows a

32
00:02:10.185 --> 00:02:15.505
single request, and one that follows a
whole cycle from activation to deactivation.

33
00:02:15.505 --> 00:02:18.594
Learn's activity list
names every event PIM logs,

34
00:02:18.594 --> 00:02:21.992
and its security operations
guide says what to watch.

35
00:02:21.992 --> 00:02:26.421
The queries only exist in the web
page, so the repository pins it,

36
00:02:26.421 --> 00:02:31.530
unmodified, and the tests run all three
over a set of fictional audit rows.

37
00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.040
It's a test, not a query engine.

38
00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:41.827
Running the queries over fictional
rows shows exactly what they catch.

39
00:02:41.827 --> 00:02:45.153
The first query finds the
activations, which is good.

40
00:02:45.153 --> 00:02:48.757
But it also sweeps in two role
assignments made outside PIM,

41
00:02:48.757 --> 00:02:52.877
so a standing Tier 0 assignment
looks like just another activation.

42
00:02:52.877 --> 00:02:57.777
It misses deactivations and the setting
change Learn says to always alert on.

43
00:02:57.777 --> 00:03:01.036
And the second query, copied
with its placeholder,

44
00:03:01.036 --> 00:03:05.803
doesn't select one request at all; it
returns everything with a request ID.

45
00:03:05.803 --> 00:03:09.958
Given a real ID, though, the
queries show the key idea:

46
00:03:09.958 --> 00:03:14.114
one request, three correlation
IDs, one activation.

47
00:03:14.114 --> 00:03:15.740
The code keeps that.

48
00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.278
The bigger gaps are about scale and proof.

49
00:03:22.278 --> 00:03:26.502
The queries look at one request at
a time, with no window, no tier,

50
00:03:26.502 --> 00:03:28.988
and no check that anyone approved it,

51
00:03:28.988 --> 00:03:33.627
and "every elevation in the period"
can't be shown one request at a time.

52
00:03:33.627 --> 00:03:35.964
They also only run in Log Analytics,

53
00:03:35.964 --> 00:03:39.428
which needs a workspace
before any evidence exists.

54
00:03:39.428 --> 00:03:42.756
And they stop at the
query: nothing compares,

55
00:03:42.756 --> 00:03:45.715
packages or hands anything to the ISSO.

56
00:03:45.715 --> 00:03:50.400
So the verdict: keep Learn's request
ID as the key and its activity names

57
00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.690
as the vocabulary, but don't treat the
first query as evidence of elevation.

58
00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.320
Now the code, in two pieces: a PowerShell
run that reads the tenant, and new

59
00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.778
Python files beside the first workbook's
drift pipeline, which stays unchanged.

60
00:04:09.778 --> 00:04:11.602
The design has three parts.

61
00:04:11.602 --> 00:04:14.616
Chapters 1 and 2 describe
the tenant's state.

62
00:04:14.616 --> 00:04:17.515
The audit read describes
the events in a window.

63
00:04:17.515 --> 00:04:21.102
And comparing with the last
run turns state into drift.

64
00:04:21.102 --> 00:04:23.256
Chain those windows with no gaps,

65
00:04:23.256 --> 00:04:26.527
and you have every elevation
in the period, in code.

66
00:04:26.527 --> 00:04:31.164
Each run writes one folder, with
a run file that records the cloud,

67
00:04:31.164 --> 00:04:34.950
tenant, window and a hash
of each task's manifest.

68
00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:42.935
The audit read asks Graph's directory
audit log for one window at a time:

69
00:04:42.935 --> 00:04:45.929
everything PIM logged,
plus the role assignments

70
00:04:45.929 --> 00:04:48.850
the directory logs when
someone goes around PIM.

71
00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:52.163
It goes through the same
careful reader as Chapter 1,

72
00:04:52.163 --> 00:04:54.783
refuses a window older than the log keeps,

73
00:04:54.783 --> 00:04:59.175
and starts each window where the last
one ended, so no hour goes unread.

74
00:04:59.175 --> 00:05:01.500
It keeps IDs and drops names.

75
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:06.249
Then four checks: a role assigned
outside PIM, a sensitive activation with

76
00:05:06.249 --> 00:05:11.450
no approval, someone approving their
own request, and a PIM setting changed.

77
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.563
Learn's pages disagree in
two places that matter here,

78
00:05:18.563 --> 00:05:23.225
and the chapter writes both down so
nobody quietly loosens the code later.

79
00:05:23.225 --> 00:05:25.983
They name PIM's logging
service differently,

80
00:05:25.983 --> 00:05:29.337
so the code asks for both
names and removes duplicates.

81
00:05:29.337 --> 00:05:31.863
And they disagree on where
approvals are logged.

82
00:05:31.863 --> 00:05:36.403
The code reads PIM's service, so if an
approval only shows up somewhere else,

83
00:05:36.403 --> 00:05:38.562
the no-approval check fires anyway.

84
00:05:38.562 --> 00:05:43.751
That's deliberate: it fails closed, giving
a false finding rather than a missed one.

85
00:05:43.751 --> 00:05:48.310
The lab in a test tenant then records
which way each one really goes.

86
00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.835
The comparison is where
state becomes drift.

87
00:05:54.835 --> 00:06:00.184
Before it compares anything, it checks both
runs: every file against its manifest,

88
00:06:00.184 --> 00:06:04.735
the same tenant, cloud and mode, and
a current run that's actually later.

89
00:06:04.735 --> 00:06:06.810
Then every difference gets a result.

90
00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.885
Two of them are careful by design.

91
00:06:08.885 --> 00:06:12.382
A task that failed this time
is unverified, not resolved,

92
00:06:12.382 --> 00:06:16.260
so a permissions error can't make
yesterday's findings vanish.

93
00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.188
And activations are left
out of assignment drift,

94
00:06:19.188 --> 00:06:23.473
because they come and go within hours
and the audit already records them.

95
00:06:23.473 --> 00:06:28.170
Any Tier 0 change is an incident;
that's the workbook's design.

96
00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.407
Two design choices protect the evidence.

97
00:06:34.407 --> 00:06:37.270
First, no names reach the issue tracker.

98
00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:42.504
Issue titles come from a fixed catalog,
bodies carry object IDs and fingerprints,

99
00:06:42.504 --> 00:06:46.332
and a test checks that no name
from either run slips through.

100
00:06:46.332 --> 00:06:49.308
Names stay in the
access-controlled evidence store.

101
00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:53.144
Second, each package records
the hash of the one before it,

102
00:06:53.144 --> 00:06:55.021
so the packages form a chain.

103
00:06:55.021 --> 00:06:59.020
Replace a package, or skip a day,
and the chain breaks visibly.

104
00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:02.903
There's no signature; the hashes
plus storage that can't be

105
00:07:02.903 --> 00:07:07.640
edited or deleted do that job, and
signing stays the ISSO's choice.

106
00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:12.803
This is the deliverable.

107
00:07:12.803 --> 00:07:16.963
For any period, the code verifies
every package, checks the chain,

108
00:07:16.963 --> 00:07:20.575
confirms the windows cover
the whole period with no gap,

109
00:07:20.575 --> 00:07:23.402
and writes a summary
laid out against Gate 4.

110
00:07:23.402 --> 00:07:25.587
It's clear about who decides what:

111
00:07:25.587 --> 00:07:29.958
the authorizing official accepts
residual standing privilege,

112
00:07:29.958 --> 00:07:33.276
the ISSO judges the
elevations, and one criterion,

113
00:07:33.276 --> 00:07:37.727
retiring old group policy settings,
simply isn't a privileged read.

114
00:07:37.727 --> 00:07:42.069
The sample workflow runs daily with
a federated sign-in and no secret,

115
00:07:42.069 --> 00:07:44.964
and assembles last month's
period on the first.

116
00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:48.340
Six-hourly runs need
only a shorter schedule.

117
00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:53.152
Who is this for?

118
00:07:53.152 --> 00:07:56.953
Developers and pipeline engineers
own the scheduled checks.

119
00:07:56.953 --> 00:08:01.012
The ISSO and the identity governance
officers receive the evidence,

120
00:08:01.012 --> 00:08:05.714
and they can check every package with
nothing more than a standard hashing tool.

121
00:08:05.714 --> 00:08:08.443
The governance officer
signs the period summary,

122
00:08:08.443 --> 00:08:12.702
and the authorizing official accepts
whatever standing privilege remains.

123
00:08:12.702 --> 00:08:15.500
The reader app holds
nine Graph permissions,

124
00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:19.203
and not one of them can write,
plus Reader on each Azure scope.

125
00:08:19.203 --> 00:08:24.070
It signs in with a certificate or a
federated credential, never a secret.

126
00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.620
Lab 1 runs on a laptop with no tenant.

127
00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:34.470
First, run Learn's queries
over the fictional audit rows.

128
00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:37.216
Eight tests pass, and
the third shows the trap:

129
00:08:37.216 --> 00:08:41.670
two of the rows the first query returns
are assignments made outside PIM.

130
00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:43.995
Next, list the run's permissions.

131
00:08:43.995 --> 00:08:46.395
There are nine, and
none of them can write.

132
00:08:46.395 --> 00:08:49.283
Then run two days against
the fictional tenant.

133
00:08:49.283 --> 00:08:53.798
The second day has three Tier 0
findings: a role assigned outside PIM,

134
00:08:53.798 --> 00:08:57.669
an activation nobody approved,
and a changed role setting.

135
00:08:57.669 --> 00:09:01.960
Open the events file, and
you'll see IDs, not names.

136
00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:10.600
The second half of the lab follows
the evidence all the way to the ISSO.

137
00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.173
Compare day one, and it's the baseline.

138
00:09:13.173 --> 00:09:18.485
Compare day two, and six incidents
appear, while six old findings resolve.

139
00:09:18.485 --> 00:09:21.496
Draft the issues, and a
search for the fictional

140
00:09:21.496 --> 00:09:24.585
domain finds nothing: no
names reach the tracker.

141
00:09:24.585 --> 00:09:28.547
Package day two, chained to
day one, and it verifies.

142
00:09:28.547 --> 00:09:32.759
Assemble the period, and the Gate
4 table shows two criteria not met,

143
00:09:32.759 --> 00:09:35.209
which is exactly right for this tenant.

144
00:09:35.209 --> 00:09:40.140
Finally, edit one file or swap
the days, and the checks refuse.

145
00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:48.828
Lab 2 uses a test tenant, never
production, and the run only reads.

146
00:09:48.828 --> 00:09:53.944
You register the reader app with the nine
permissions, make one test activation

147
00:09:53.944 --> 00:09:58.440
of a low-tier role, and run the evidence
twice, once before and once after.

148
00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.596
Then comes the real point of the lab:
checking what the fixtures assumed,

149
00:10:02.596 --> 00:10:07.315
like which service name PIM's events carry
and whether the request ID is filled in.

150
00:10:07.315 --> 00:10:10.136
Cross-check against PIM's own audit view,

151
00:10:10.136 --> 00:10:14.153
as Learn describes, then compare
and package the two runs.

152
00:10:14.153 --> 00:10:18.200
Back it out, and in production,
lock the retention policy.

153
00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:24.613
Every danger here is a
way evidence can lie.

154
00:10:24.613 --> 00:10:27.704
The quietest is a failed
read that looks like a fix:

155
00:10:27.704 --> 00:10:31.474
a permissions error, and
yesterday's findings simply vanish.

156
00:10:31.474 --> 00:10:35.350
The code marks the task as an error
and keeps those findings open.

157
00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:37.534
Close behind is a day nobody read,

158
00:10:37.534 --> 00:10:40.688
which would let a gate pass
on the days that were.

159
00:10:40.688 --> 00:10:44.645
Others are about trust: names
leaking into the tracker,

160
00:10:44.645 --> 00:10:48.338
a run edited or swapped, a
package quietly replaced.

161
00:10:48.338 --> 00:10:52.034
And one is about attention:
count every activation as drift,

162
00:10:52.034 --> 00:10:55.100
and people stop reading
the incident queue.

163
00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.200
Here's what done looks like.

164
00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.890
On the run side: all three tasks succeed,

165
00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:08.438
the reader holds exactly nine read-only
permissions and no secret, every window

166
00:11:08.438 --> 00:11:13.313
picks up where the last one ended, and
every Tier 0 incident has a record.

167
00:11:13.313 --> 00:11:16.146
On the evidence side: no
names in the tracker,

168
00:11:16.146 --> 00:11:21.028
every package verifies with a standard
hashing tool, the chain is unbroken,

169
00:11:21.028 --> 00:11:25.437
the storage is locked, and the period
summary is signed and accepted.

170
00:11:25.437 --> 00:11:28.912
Next is Chapter 9, deploying
to test and production.

171
00:11:28.912 --> 00:11:30.070
Thanks for watching.
